age-proof(1) -- accountable provenance for age encrypted files
================================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`age-proof sign` (`-k` <SIGNER>)... [`--armor`] [`--expires` <RFC3339>] [`-o` <OUT>] [<INPUT>...]<br>
`age-proof verify` (`-t` <TRUSTLIST>)... [`--list`] [<INPUT>...]<br>
`age-proof decrypt` (`-t` <TRUSTLIST>)... [`-i` <IDENTITY>]... [`--require-proof`] [`-o` <OUT>] [<INPUT>...]<br>
`age-proof unwrap` [`-o` <OUT>] [<INPUT>]<br>
`age-proof keygen` [`-y`] [`-o` <OUT>] [<INPUT>]<br>

## DESCRIPTION

`age-proof` adds an accountable provenance layer (the `age-proof-v1`
envelope) around an existing age encrypted file, without changing a single
byte of the inner age ciphertext. The envelope carries one or more Ed25519
signatures that cover every inner byte through SHA-256, together with the
inner length and the signer's key id. Files that carry no envelope keep
working exactly as before; files that carry one are verified against a
receiver-maintained trust list before any plaintext is produced.

The identity of a signer is established solely by the receiver's trust
list; the envelope never carries a self-asserted name. Verification is
streaming and memory-bounded: header parsing is capped, payload processing
uses a fixed 32 KiB buffer, and no plaintext is written until every
signature, the length and the digest have all validated. A rejected
envelope produces no output and leaves no partial file.

A reader that does not understand the layer can use `age-proof unwrap` to
recover the exact inner age file and decrypt it with an unmodified age(1)
build.

## SUBCOMMANDS

* `sign`:
    Wrap each age encrypted <INPUT> in a provenance envelope signed with
    every `-k`/`--key` signer. Armored age input is de-armored first so the
    signed bytes are always the canonical binary age ciphertext. Plaintext,
    empty files, and files that already carry an envelope are rejected.

    Directories are processed recursively. In directory mode outputs are
    written next to their inputs (`.age.proof` for binary envelopes,
    `.age.aproof` for armored ones) and a failure on one file never stops
    the others; the exit code is 8 if any input failed.

* `verify`:
    Check every <INPUT> against the merged trust lists. With `--list`,
    each verified signer's fingerprint and trust-list label is reported.

* `decrypt`:
    Verify the envelope (if present) against the trust lists, then decrypt
    the inner age file with the given identities, or prompt for a
    passphrase when none are supplied. The output file is created only
    after verification succeeds. With `--require-proof`, files without an
    envelope are rejected instead of handled as legacy files.

* `unwrap`:
    Extract the exact inner age ciphertext without verifying it. Intended
    only for interop with readers that lack provenance support.

* `keygen`:
    Generate a new Ed25519 signer key in OpenSSH PEM format, or with `-y`
    derive the public key (both the `ssh-ed25519` and the native
    `age-proof-1` form) from <INPUT>.

## TRUST LIST FORMAT

A UTF-8 text file, one entry per line; blank lines and lines starting with
`#` are ignored. A missing, unreadable, or empty trust list is always a
failure, never an implicit accept.

    ssh-ed25519 AAAA... optional label
    age-proof-1... optional label
    revoked: <key line>
    expires: 2030-01-01T00:00:00Z <key line>

## EXIT STATUS

| Code | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| 0 | success |
| 1 | generic failure (usage, I/O, age decryption) |
| 2 | trust list unreadable or empty |
| 3 | no provenance envelope present |
| 4 | envelope malformed, signature invalid, digest/length mismatch, truncation, append, or cross-file transplant |
| 5 | signer not in the trust list |
| 6 | signer revoked in the trust list |
| 7 | proof or trust entry expired |
| 8 | batch processing completed with one or more per-file failures |

## SECURITY PROPERTIES

* Each signature binds the SHA-256 and length of every inner byte, so any
  single-bit change (header, nonce area, chunk boundary, length field, or
  payload) fails verification before output starts.
* Signer identity comes only from the local trust list.
* Passphrases, private keys, and plaintext never appear in log lines or
  error messages.
* Non-seekable input is staged in a 0600 temporary file holding only
  ciphertext; plaintext is never spooled.
* The outer envelope uses a distinct PEM type (`AGE PROVENANCE FILE`) and
  a distinct binary magic, so it cannot be mistaken for or pollute the
  inner age format.

## EXAMPLE

    $ age-proof keygen -o signer.txt
    $ age -r age1... -o report.tar.gz.age report.tar.gz
    $ age-proof sign -k signer.txt --armor -o report.tar.gz.age.aproof report.tar.gz.age
    $ age-proof verify -t trusted-signers.txt report.tar.gz.age.aproof
    $ age-proof decrypt -t trusted-signers.txt -i identity.txt -o report.tar.gz report.tar.gz.age.aproof

## SEE ALSO

age(1), age-keygen(1)
